;
; Manchester (biphase) transmitters for the MVP chip.
;
; Two programs, one per chip split, because the split decides which bit rates the wire can
; legally carry.  Both are the same shape -- pull, sync preamble, eight data bits of two
; instructions each, an idle gap, and back to the top -- and both are assembled by
; asm/pioasm.py; fw/manchester/driver.py picks one by name and derives the CLKDIV from the
; words.
;
;   .program manchester        5 SM cycles per bit: 10BASE-T, 10 Mbit/s at CLKDIV = 1.0
;   .program manchester_even   4 SM cycles per bit: the two chips of a cell are the same
;                              length, so every bit rate this divider can express is exact
;
; The chip is the *transmitter* on one push-pull pin; the independent judge is
; verif/vip/manchester.py (ManchesterRx + ManchesterTimingChecker), written from the
; specifications saved in research/ref/protocols/manchester/.  One 32-bit TX word carries
; one 8-bit message: the program frames it with the two-bit sync preamble AN2358 asks for,
; so the host pushes plain bytes and nothing else.
;
; The line code, and why two instructions per data bit
; ---------------------------------------------------
; IEEE 802.3 rises in the middle of a 1 and falls in the middle of a 0 (AN1120a: "In
; Ethernet, a logical '0' is encoded as a high-to-low transition, while a logical '1' is
; encode as a low-to-high transition"), so one bit is two half-bit level periods -- two
; "chips" in AN3435's word -- of the line:
;
;       bit 1 ->  low  then high          bit 0 ->  high then low
;
; The mid-bit transition is what makes the signal self-clocking and it is mandatory in
; every cell (AN2358: "each bit is transmitted with a transition in the middle of bit
; time").  A transition *at a bit boundary* is inserted only when it has to be, because
; the line has to be in the right level for the next cell's mid-bit transition: AN2358
; "additional transitions are inserted between bits to satisfy the mid-bit transition
; rule".  Both halves of that fall out of the chip list below without the program ever
; deciding anything:
;
;       chip 2k    = the first half of bit k   = NOT b_k
;       chip 2k+1  = the second half of bit k  = b_k
;       boundary between cells k and k+1: a transition iff NOT b_k != NOT b_{k+1},
;                                                 i.e. iff b_k == b_{k+1}
;
; So the encoder is "the two halves of a cell are the bit and its complement", and the
; boundary rule is a consequence of that, not a rule this program has to implement.  Two
; instructions per bit, both writing the one-pin OUT window:
;
;       mov pins, osr !     writes ~OSR, whose bit 0 is ~b_k: the first half.  A MOV never
;                           stalls and never shifts, so the bit is still there.
;       out pins, 1         writes OSR bit 0 = b_k: the second half, and shifts the OSR
;                           right by one so the next iteration sees b_{k+1}.
;
; There is no way to do it in one: the only instruction that shifts the OSR is OUT, and the
; only way to write a pin level from a register *inverted* is MOV with the invert Op
; (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Field values`, MOV Op `01`).  Sixteen data bits would therefore need
; 32 words before any loop overhead, which does not fit the 32-word IMEM, so one word is
; one 8-bit message.
;
; What decides the bit rate: the length of the two chips
; ------------------------------------------------------
; Three instructions make a bit -- the two chip writes and the loop's `jmp` -- and the pin
; holds its level through all three, so the chips are
;
;       first chip  = 1 + delay(`mov pins, osr !`)  SM cycles
;       second chip = 1 + delay(`out pins, 1`) + 1  SM cycles   (the `jmp` holds it)
;       bit         = the sum of those
;
; and CLKDIV then scales one SM cycle into core cycles.  The line code wants the two chips
; of a cell to be *equal* (half a bit each), so how far off that split may be is a question
; about the receiver, and the answer is the half-bit window of the judge:
; `verif/vip/manchester.py` accepts a measured interval inside
; [half - tol, half + tol] for a bit-boundary transition, with
; tol = max(1 tick, 10 % of a half bit) -- AN2358's ">10 percent frequency tolerance on
; each side" plus the one tick that `core.py`'s tick grid costs.
;
;   .program manchester      5 SM cycles per bit, chips 3 and 2: half a bit is 2.5, so each
;                            chip is 0.5 SM cycles (0.5 * CLKDIV ticks) off a half bit.
;                            Legal while that stays inside the tolerance: at CLKDIV = 1.0
;                            that is the 10BASE-T rate of 10 Mbit/s (AN1120a's 100 ns bit
;                            time, and the fastest this judge can classify at all: its
;                            half-bit window [1.5, 3.5] ticks and bit window [4, 6] stop
;                            being disjoint at 12.5 Mbit/s, and `profile_timings` raises
;                            rather than guess).  At CLKDIV = 2.0 (5 Mbit/s) the error is
;                            exactly the one-tick allowance, and at CLKDIV = 3.0 or more it
;                            is outside it -- so this program reaches 10 and 5 Mbit/s and
;                            no slower rate.
;
;   .program manchester_even 4 SM cycles per bit, chips 2 and 2: exact at every rate, so
;                            the wire is on the judge's bit grid to the tick whatever
;                            CLKDIV is.  The ceiling is CLKDIV = 1.0 again, 12.5 Mbit/s,
;                            but that is exactly the rate the judge refuses to classify, so
;                            the fastest *provable* rate is CLKDIV = 2.0 = 6.25 Mbit/s --
;                            and from there every rate whose bit period is a multiple of
;                            four ticks, which includes 100 kbit/s (CLKDIV = 125) and
;                            AN1470a's worked example of 25 kbit/s (CLKDIV = 500).
;
; The sync preamble, and the phase it has to leave behind
; ------------------------------------------------------
; There is no start bit in this line code, so a receiver anchors on the first transition of
; a stream and takes it for a mid-bit transition.  That is only right if the first chip on
; the wire is the level the line was already at, which is what AN2358's rule is: "always
; start messages with a '0' bit when the idle state is low. Or, send a 0 to 1 (or 1 to 0)
; preamble to guarantee a correct synchronization in any situation".  The line idles high
; here (the profile's default), so the preamble is the pair 0, 1 -- whose two cells are the
; chip list 1, 0, 0, 1: no transition at the first chip (it is the idle level), a falling
; mid-bit transition for the first preamble bit, no transition between the cells, and a
; rising one for the second.
;
; Two `set pins` instructions make that, with no OSR involved, and their delays are what
; put the *data* bits back on the judge's bit grid -- the grid is anchored on the preamble's
; first mid-bit transition and advances by exactly one bit time, so the first data bit's
; mid-bit transition has to land two bit times after it:
;
;       set pins, 0 [PRE_LOW_HOLD]   level 0 for the preamble's bit-0 second half and its
;                                    bit-1 first half: exactly one bit time, so PRE_LOW_HOLD
;                                    is (SM cycles per bit - 1).
;       set pins, 1                  level 1, no delay.  This one instruction and the `set x`
;                                    below it are the preamble's last cell's second half --
;                                    a half bit, two SM cycles -- and then the data loop's
;                                    first chip starts on the cell boundary.
;       set x, (DATA_BITS - 1)       the counter, in the middle of that half bit, which is
;                                    why it must be exactly one instruction there.
;
; Getting that half bit one SM cycle wrong is invisible at 10 Mbit/s (the judge's grid
; tolerance is one tick, and the error would be one tick) and a violation at any slower
; rate, so the driver checks the phase instead of trusting it; see `message_sm_cycles` and
; `fw/manchester/README.md`.
;
; The preamble is also what makes the G.E. Thomas convention nearly free: its preamble is
; the complement pair 1, 0, which drives the same two chip levels in the same two slots, and
; its data chips are the complement of IEEE 802.3's -- so the host pushes the complement of
; each byte (`fw/manchester/driver.py`, `convention="thomas"`) and the program, unchanged,
; emits a Thomas stream.  See README.md.
;
; Host configuration (docs/mvp-regmap.md `### Per-engine registers`), all of it built by
; fw/manchester/driver.py:
;
;   SHIFTCTRL  OUT_SHIFTDIR = 1 (its reset value: shift right, so the least significant
;              data bit leaves first), AUTOPULL = 1 and PULL_THRESH = 8: the eight
;              `out pins, 1` of a message empty the OSR and the eighth refills it from the
;              TX FIFO in the same SM cycle, so the `pull block` at the top of the loop is
;              a no-op barrier in steady state and never stalls (docs/mvp-isa.md
;              `### Instruction semantics`, PULL).
;   PINCTRL    SIDESET_COUNT = 0.  These programs have no `.side_set`, so all five bits of
;              bits 12:8 are delay -- and the *reset* value of SIDESET_COUNT is 2, which
;              with the reset EXECCTRL.SIDE_EN = 0 would make every instruction side-set
;              level 0 into SIDESET_BASE and SIDESET_BASE+1, and the side-set channel beats
;              OUT/SET on the same pin (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Pin writes`), holding the
;              line low.  OUT_BASE = SET_BASE = the TX pin, OUT_COUNT = SET_COUNT = 1, so
;              both channels only ever touch that one pin.
;   EXECCTRL   SIDE_EN = 0, WRAP_BOTTOM = 0, WRAP_TOP = the last instruction below.
;   CLKDIV     the 16.8 divider, derived from the SM cycles per bit the words above add up
;              to (four for manchester_even, five for manchester).
;
; A TX pin has to be one of the bidirectional uio pins, 0..7, because that is where PINDIRS
; can drive a level (docs/mvp-isa.md `### The 18 protocol pins`); Manchester is push-pull,
; so there is no open-drain sense question here.
;
; ================================================================================================
; .program manchester: 5 SM cycles per bit, chips of 3 and 2.
;
;   mov pins, osr ! [2]   the first chip is 1 + 2 = 3 SM cycles
;   out pins, 1           the second chip is 1 + 0 + 1 (the `jmp`) = 2 SM cycles
;   jmp x-- bitloop       3 + 2 = 5 SM cycles per bit, i.e. 5 ticks at CLKDIV = 1.0
;
; That is AN1120a's 10BASE-T rate: 5 ticks of 20 ns is a 100 ns bit time, 10 Mbit/s, the
; profile `verif/vip/manchester.py` defaults to.  The cost is the split: a half bit is 2.5
; ticks and these chips are 3 and 2, so each is half a tick off, which is inside the judge's
; one-tick tolerance at CLKDIV = 1.0 and exactly on it at CLKDIV = 2.0 (5 Mbit/s).
; ================================================================================================
.program manchester

.define DATA_BITS 8           ; one message is one byte
.define PRE_LOW_HOLD 4        ; 1 + 4 = 5 SM cycles of level 0: one bit time
.define IDLE_HOLD 23          ; 1 + 23 = 24 SM cycles of idle line between messages

PUBLIC start:
        set pins, 1                    ; the TX pin's level register comes out of reset at
                                       ; 0, so it has to be told the idle level before
                                       ; anything drives it, and in that order -- see
                                       ; README.md for the runt edge the other order makes.
        set pindirs, 1                 ; the pin becomes a push-pull output

message:
        pull block                     ; OSR <- the next byte from the TX FIFO, in bits
                                       ; 7:0.  Autopull refilled the OSR at the end of the
                                       ; last message, so in steady state this is a no-op
                                       ; barrier; with an empty FIFO it stalls here while
                                       ; the line is still idle, which is where a
                                       ; transmitter waits for its host.
        set pins, 0 [PRE_LOW_HOLD]     ; the sync preamble: falling mid-bit transition of
                                       ; its first bit, no transition between the two
                                       ; cells, and the first half of the second cell
        set pins, 1                    ; rising mid-bit transition of the preamble's
                                       ; second bit.  No delay: this instruction and the
                                       ; `set x` below it are that cell's second half.
        set x, (DATA_BITS - 1)         ; eight data bits: the eighth `jmp x--` falls
                                       ; through

bitloop:
        mov pins, osr ! [2]            ; first half of the cell: ~b, and no shift, so the
                                       ; bit is still there for the instruction below
        out pins, 1                    ; second half: b, and the next bit arrives
        jmp x-- bitloop                ; seven more bits, then fall through

        set pins, 1 [IDLE_HOLD]        ; the line rests at the idle level, long enough for
                                       ; the judge's minimum gap between messages (four
                                       ; bit times) with room to spare.  A `set` and not an
                                       ; `out`, so the OSR keeps the next word.
        jmp message

; ================================================================================================
; .program manchester_even: 4 SM cycles per bit, chips of 2 and 2.
;
;   mov pins, osr ! [1]   the first chip is 1 + 1 = 2 SM cycles
;   out pins, 1           the second chip is 1 + 0 + 1 (the `jmp`) = 2 SM cycles
;   jmp x-- bitloop       2 + 2 = 4 SM cycles per bit, half of each exactly
;
; Identical to the program above except for three delay digits, and that is the whole
; point: with equal chips the wire sits on the judge's bit grid to the tick at every CLKDIV,
; instead of half a tick off at CLKDIV = 1.0 and a whole tick off -- the whole allowance --
; at CLKDIV = 2.0.  The ceiling is therefore lower (12.5 Mbit/s at CLKDIV = 1.0, of which
; the judge can only certify 6.25 Mbit/s) but the range is far wider: anything whose bit
; period is a multiple of four ticks, so 6.25, 4.1667, 2.5, 1.25, 0.625, 0.5, 0.25, 0.1 and
; 0.025 Mbit/s (CLKDIV = 2, 3, 5, 10, 20, 25, 50, 125, 500).
;
; The sync preamble's split changes with it: one bit time of level 0 is now four SM cycles,
; so PRE_LOW_HOLD is 3, and `set pins, 1` plus `set x` are still exactly one half bit of
; level 1 (two SM cycles) with the first data chip starting on the cell boundary.
; ================================================================================================
.program manchester_even

.define DATA_BITS 8
.define PRE_LOW_HOLD 3
.define IDLE_HOLD 23

PUBLIC start:
        set pins, 1
        set pindirs, 1

message:
        pull block
        set pins, 0 [PRE_LOW_HOLD]
        set pins, 1
        set x, (DATA_BITS - 1)

bitloop:
        mov pins, osr ! [1]
        out pins, 1
        jmp x-- bitloop

        set pins, 1 [IDLE_HOLD]
        jmp message
